œ#­1. I have briefly set forth what I thought necessary about the principles of hoisting machines. In them two different things, unlike each other, work together, as elements of their motion and power, to produce these effects. One of them is the right line, which the Greeks term Îµá½‘Î¸ÎµÎ¹Î±; the other is the circle, which the Greeks call ÎºÏ…ÎºÎ»Ï‰Ï„á¼¡; but in point of fact, neither rectilinear without circular motion, nor revolutions, without rectilinear motion, can accomplish the raising of loads. I will explain this, so that it may be understood.
